<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Deliberately almost empty, and that is the finding rather than an oversight.

    Notarisation needs the hardened runtime, and the worry recorded on CP-239 was that the hardened
    runtime would block the app from spawning `node`. It does not: the hardened runtime constrains
    what THIS process loads into itself — library validation, JIT, DYLD variables — and a child
    process is a separate process with its own signature. So no entitlement is needed to run node.

    Two that are conspicuously absent, on purpose:

    com.apple.security.app-sandbox
      Not sandboxed. A sandboxed app could not spawn an arbitrary node, could not read the
      operator's checkout, and could not write ~/.claudeplanner. Sandboxing this would mean
      redesigning what the app is for.

    com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
      Not needed, and not added speculatively. Nothing is loaded into this process at runtime.
      Adding it "just in case" weakens the app and is exactly what notarisation review asks about.
  -->
</dict>
</plist>
